            "text": "There is a particular kind of wilderness that survives in the cracks of a city. It does not announce itself with mountains or magnificent rivers. It arrives as a dandelion beside a parking meter, a moth orbiting the laundromat light, a small tree growing from a building nobody remembers owning.\n\nWe tend to call these things weeds, pests, or maintenance problems. Sometimes they are. Roots can break pavement; pigeons are not thoughtful neighbors. But there is something oddly reassuring about life refusing to wait for an invitation. The world keeps making little attempts, even where we have declared it finished.\n\nImagine a seed landing in a teaspoon of dirt on a rooftop. Its circumstances are absurd. There is almost nothing to work with, and the wind is rude. Still, given rain and a little warmth, it begins the ancient business of becoming something larger than the package it arrived in.\n\nI like the idea that attention can change the size of a place. A street you hurry through is one block long. The same street, examined closely, contains migrations, weather systems, rivalries, shelter, hunger, and improbable victories. Nothing has been added except your willingness to notice what was already happening.\n\nThis is not an argument against grand adventures. Go see the ocean if you can. Stand beneath enormous trees. But on an ordinary Tuesday, when the bus is late, look down. Somewhere near your shoe, a green thing may be lifting its entire small life toward a scrap of sky."
          }
